# IsdaYou — Updated Build Checklist

**What changed and why:** the manuscript specifies Android 9.0+ as the minimum OS (Section 2.4) and an architecture where identification, the species library, catch logging, and geofencing all run **on-device with no network connection** (Section 12). That's a native Android app, not a responsive website. The original checklist's step 7 built a React website — replaced below with native Android setup. A web frontend only exists for the **admin console** used by fisheries personnel (kept, but separated out and marked "web, admin only").

---

## 1. Set up GitHub (source code storage)
1.1. Create a GitHub account (if you don't have one) at github.com
1.2. Create a new repository, name it "isdayou"
1.3. Set it to Private
1.4. Add your teammates as collaborators (Settings → Collaborators → add by username or email)
1.5. Clone the repo to your computer (`git clone [link]`)
1.6. Set up three top-level folders in the repo: `mobile-app/` (the Android app), `backend/` (API + sync + admin), `ml/` (training scripts, datasets, exported models)

## 2. Install your coding tools

**For the mobile app (Android — this is the core deliverable):**
2.1. Download and install **Android Studio** (developer.android.com/studio) — the IDE for building the Android app; it bundles the Android SDK and an emulator
2.2. Install a **JDK** if Android Studio doesn't bundle one automatically (Android Studio now ships its own, usually no separate install needed)
2.3. In Android Studio, install an emulator image matching your minimum target (Android 9.0 / API 28) and, if possible, also test on a real budget Android phone — the emulator doesn't tell you much about real camera/GPS/performance behavior

**For the backend, sync API, and ML training:**
2.4. Download and install **Git** (git-scm.com)
2.5. Download and install **Python 3.11+** (python.org) — backend API and model training
2.6. Download and install **Node.js LTS** (nodejs.org) — only needed for the web-based admin console, not the mobile app
2.7. Download and install **Docker Desktop** (docker.com) — runs your database without installing it directly
2.8. Download and install **VS Code** (code.visualstudio.com) — for backend/Python/admin-console code (Android app code itself is written in Android Studio, not VS Code)
2.9. In VS Code, install the "Python" extension and the "ESLint" extension

## 3. Find and prepare your fish dataset
3.1. Decide how many fish species you want to start with (recommend 10–16; the manuscript's indicative list prioritizes species under closed seasons, size limits, or protection)
3.2. Search for existing labeled fish image datasets (Kaggle, Roboflow Universe — search "fish species Philippines" or "fish classification dataset")
3.3. Check the BFAR website for official species references or photos you can use
3.4. If you can't find enough images online, take your own photos at markets or landing sites (ask permission from vendors/fisherfolk; don't include identifiable faces in the shots)
3.5. Organize images into folders, one per species (e.g., `/tilapia/`, `/bangus/`, `/galunggong/`)
3.6. Make a CSV listing each species: common name, scientific name, local name, whether it's restricted, size limit, closed season if any — this feeds both model class labels and the offline species-library content

## 4. Set up your AI tool (YOLOv11) — including mobile export
4.1. Install the Ultralytics package: `pip install ultralytics` — no account needed to run it locally
4.2. Follow their quickstart guide to confirm it works using their sample dataset first
4.3. Once confirmed, replace the sample data with your organized fish photo folders from step 3
4.4. Train, then **export the trained model to TFLite** (`model.export(format="tflite")`), since a `.pt` PyTorch file cannot run inside an Android app
4.5. Apply INT8 post-training quantization on export to shrink the model and speed up inference on entry-level phones — then re-check accuracy on your test set, since quantized accuracy is not guaranteed to match the full-precision model
4.6. Keep a versioned class-index file (species name ↔ class number) alongside every exported model — a mismatched index silently produces confident wrong species names

## 5. Set up your database
5.1. In `backend/`, create a `docker-compose.yml`
5.2. Set up **PostgreSQL with the PostGIS extension** — this is the authoritative backend database for species/regulation/geofence records; it is not what runs on the phone
5.3. Run `docker compose up -d` to start it
5.4. Confirm it's running by connecting with DBeaver (dbeaver.io)

## 6. Set up the backend (sync API + admin data)
6.1. In `backend/`, run `python -m venv venv` to create a Python workspace
6.2. Activate it, then `pip install fastapi uvicorn sqlalchemy psycopg2-binary`
6.3. Create `main.py` with a simple "Hello World" API
6.4. Run `uvicorn main:app --reload`, check `localhost:8000`
6.5. This backend's job is narrow: serve species/regulation/geofence data to the app on sync, and receive queued catch/identification logs when the phone reconnects. It is **not** where fish identification happens — that runs on the device.

## 7. Set up the mobile app (Android — where users actually scan fish)
7.1. In Android Studio, create a new project inside `mobile-app/`: **Empty Activity**, language **Kotlin**, minimum SDK **API 28 (Android 9.0)**
7.2. Add these dependencies in `build.gradle`:
   - **CameraX** — camera capture for scanning fish
   - **TensorFlow Lite Android** (`org.tensorflow:tensorflow-lite`) — runs the exported YOLO model on-device
   - **Room** — local SQLite database wrapper for the offline species library, catch logs, and identification history
   - **Retrofit + OkHttp** — talks to the FastAPI backend only when online (sync)
   - **Play Services Location** (Fused Location Provider) — GPS coordinates
   - **Play Services Location — Geofencing API** — restricted-zone entry/proximity detection
   - **WorkManager** — schedules background sync and queued-log uploads for whenever connectivity returns
   - **Jetpack Compose** (or XML layouts, either is fine) — UI
7.3. Copy the exported `.tflite` model and class-index file from step 4 into `mobile-app/app/src/main/assets/`
7.4. Build a minimal screen that opens the camera, captures a photo, and confirms the model loads and runs — before wiring up the rest of the UI
7.5. Run it on the emulator, then on a real low-spec Android phone if you have one — camera and GPS behavior on an emulator is not representative

## 8. Set up the admin console (web, admin only — not what fishers use)
8.1. In `backend/`, or a separate `admin-console/` folder, run `npm create vite@latest . -- --template react`
8.2. Run `npm install`, then `npm run dev` to confirm the starter page loads
8.3. This is where fisheries personnel manage species records, geofence boundaries, and regulation entries (FR-019 to FR-023) — it's a browser page for administrators, never installed on a fisher's phone

## 9. Save your progress
9.1. `git add .`
9.2. `git commit -m "Initial project setup"`
9.3. `git push`
9.4. Confirm your teammates can see the update on GitHub

## 10. Start building features (once steps 1–9 are done)
10.1. Build the Android login/register screens (Kotlin + your backend's auth endpoints)
10.2. Build the offline fish library — a Room-backed list of species + info, bundled or synced once, readable with zero connectivity
10.3. Wire the "scan fish" button to CameraX capture → preprocessing → the on-device TFLite model → result screen with bounding box, confidence score, and ranked alternatives
10.4. Build the online-only features: map (restricted zones), GPS-based geofencing alerts, sync
10.5. Build the admin console screens for species/geofence/regulation management (React, backend-connected)

## 11. Test everything thoroughly
11.1. Test the AI scan feature with fish photos it hasn't seen before, not just training photos — check accuracy per species, not just overall
11.2. Test the Android app fully offline (airplane mode) — confirm scanning, the fish library, and saved history all still work with zero network
11.3. Test the online-only features — confirm GPS, restricted-area alerts, and map load correctly with internet
11.4. Test going from offline → online → offline again — confirm queued logs sync and nothing is lost or duplicated
11.5. Have each team member test on their own physical Android phone, not just the emulator — camera quality, GPU/CPU, and RAM vary a lot on budget devices
11.6. Track every bug in GitHub's "Issues" tab

## 12. Fix bugs and improve based on testing
12.1. Work through the bug list from step 11.6
12.2. If the model confuses two species, add more training photos for those two and retrain (steps 3–4)
12.3. If the app feels slow or clunky on a low-end device, profile it in Android Studio before assuming it's the model's fault — inference time, UI rendering, and I/O are separate bottlenecks

## 13. Test with real users (usability testing)
13.1. Ask people outside your team — classmates, family, ideally actual fishers — to try the app on a real phone
13.2. Watch them use it without helping; note where they get confused
13.3. Ask afterward: was scanning easy? did they understand the alerts? anything unclear?
13.4. Fix anything that comes up repeatedly

## 14. Write your documentation
14.1. Technical document: tools used, system architecture, how to build and run both the Android app and the backend
14.2. User guide: how a fisher would actually use the app, step by step, in plain language
14.3. Screenshots of the Android app (not the admin console) for the user-facing parts of your documentation

## 15. Prepare your final presentation
15.1. Update slides with what was actually built vs. originally planned
15.2. Include screenshots or a short screen recording of the Android app working, both offline and online
15.3. Prepare to explain AI results — accuracy, on how many species, and where it struggles
15.4. Practice explaining the offline/online split clearly — that's the project's core selling point, and it only makes sense once people understand this is a native app, not a website

## 16. Final submission
16.1. Make sure your GitHub repo is clean and organized (remove test files, add a README)
16.2. Confirm everything is pushed and up to date
16.3. Submit according to what your instructor requires (repo link, documentation, presentation, APK if requested, etc.)

